 ## Background
-The concept of custom shading in volume rendering originated with the VolPack library, which provided a standard shading model but allowed users to override it via callback routines or custom lookup table initialization [S1]. The VolPack User's Guide details that users could define a callback routine (e.g., `vpSetCallback(vpc, VP_GRAY_SHADE_FUNC, myshader)`) to compute colors per voxel, though this could lead to significant performance degradation [S1]. This flexibility laid the groundwork for modern custom shading models in ray marching, where developers implement complex BRDFs (like GGX), voxel-specific pipelines, and procedural materials directly in shader code or CPU routines.
+The concept of custom shading in volume rendering originated with early volume rendering libraries like VolPack, which provided mechanisms for users to define custom shading lookup tables or callback routines to compute voxel colors [S1]. VolPack allowed users to initialize their own shading tables of any size or define a callback routine via `vpSetCallback()` to shade each voxel during rendering [S1]. This approach enabled complex material definitions but was noted to lead to significant performance degradation during rendering [S1].
+
+In modern real-time rendering, custom shading has evolved into specialized pipelines for specific media, such as voxel engines (e.g., Minecraft-style games) and Signed Distance Field (SDF) ray marching. Techniques like Fast Shading for Voxel Engines optimize shading by operating on palette indices rather than continuous radiance [S1]. Similarly, SDF-based renderers have adopted microfacet models (like GGX) and metallic reflections to achieve physically-based rendering results [S2, S3, unofficial]. The family also includes techniques for atmospheric phenomena, such as skylight approximation for clouds, which extends shading to multiple light sources to simulate global illumination [S4, unofficial]. Additionally, traditional isosurface rendering has utilized custom shading via triangle subdivision to improve smoothness, though this is computationally costly [S5].
 
